    10	## 1. What a client gets
    11	
    12	The client sends bytes. Tessera returns a **receipt**: a bundle that anyone can check later, without Tessera
    13	running and without our help.
    14	
    15	## 2. Guarantees
    16	
    17	**Conditions.** Every guarantee assumes the registered cryptographic, key-use, authority, chain-history and
    18	distribution assumptions (A9:32–52, A9:128–136). In particular: nobody misused the service's signing capability,
    19	SHA-256 and OpenTimestamps hold, and the checker's verifier release is authentic.
    20	
    21	When those hold, a checker who has the receipt, the published verifier and its trust configuration can establish:
    22	
    23	| # | Guarantee | Source |
    24	|---|---|---|
    25	| G1 | **Exact bytes.** The receipt binds these exact bytes. Change one bit and the check fails. | C1 |
    26	| G2 | **Presented to Tessera.** The bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service at some issuance. | C2 |
    27	| G3 | **Both signatures.** Both of the service's keys signed. A missing, stripped or swapped signature fails. | C3 |
    28	| G4 | **The keys were Tessera's.** DNSSEC (`_tessera-v1.wamason.com`, at the snapshot's own anchored time) and a signed git commit (at its own evidence time) named the keys for the service's domain. The git commit was in the Bitcoin chain at least 6 blocks before the receipt. | C4, A10.6 |
    29	| G5 | **Existed by a block.** The signed bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block: the lowest-height qualifying block in the presented proof, confirmed 6 deep. | C5, A10.1, A10.5 |
    30	| G6 | **A real-time bound, under an unchecked assumption.** A-UTC says at least one of that block and its 5 successors has a time stamp at most 2 hours behind when it was made. If A-UTC holds, the bytes existed before U (the latest of those 6 times, plus 2 hours). U is reported separately and never changes the verdict. | A10.1.1 |
    31	| G7 | **Window, two checks only.** The declared issue time is not before the keys' window opened, and the chain time T is not after it closed. This does not prove the signing happened inside the window. | C4, A10.2 |
    32	| G8 | **No Tessera needed.** The verdict depends only on the inputs above. | C6 |
    33	| G9 | **A typed verdict.** Once the trust configuration is authenticated, the verifier returns `VALID_STRICT`, `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE`, with a reason code and `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`, `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`, `standing: ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`. Missing required evidence prevents acceptance. A valid receipt still leaves everything in §3 unestablished. If the trust configuration is not authenticated, there is no receipt verdict. | C7; A9.5 trust-configuration authority; V1-PROFILE §5 (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`) |
    34	| G10 | **Honest receipts cannot be moved.** If an attacker takes over the DNS and git authority records while the service's own keys stay secret and unmisused, it cannot move honestly signed frames to other keys or bytes, or change their signer sets, whether they were signed before or after the takeover. | A10.4 |
    35	**No G11.** The service-key listing (A10.4.1) is not in V1, by the author's ruling of 2026-10-23 (E9 row 2,
    36	`docs/reviews/e9-packet/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md`; `docs/decisions/2026-10-19-listing-cut.md`). G10 holds while the independent release boundary of A9.5 holds.
    37	**New receipts under an attacker's own keys pass when both authority channels are captured.**
    38	
    39	## 3. Not guaranteed
    40	
    41	Each of these is a deliberate limit, not a missing feature.
    42	
    43	- **Who submitted the bytes**, or that a replayed receipt suits the current transaction (case f).
    44	- **What the bytes mean**, or that they are true. A contract inside the bytes is the checker's to verify (cases i, i′).
    45	- **That a key was not revoked** (cases e, e′).
    46	- **That an algorithm or a key was still safe** when it signed.
    47	- **Any lower time bound or priority.** "Existed by" is never "existed after" or "came first".
    48	- **Real time without A-UTC.** Without it, only chain order is claimed.
    49	- **Standing** of any kind.
    50	- **Separate control of the two keys.** One controller holds both. Capturing the issuing AWS account means both
    51	  slots can sign.
    52	- **Visibility.** Publishing the authority records is the issuer's duty; the verifier does not check it. V1
    53	  guarantees no detection or public exposure of a capture, of new receipts, or of a release given to one party.
    54	- **That DNSSEC shows publication before issue.**
    55	- **Anything after a Bitcoin reorganization of 6 or more blocks.**
    56	- **Renewal, degraded modes, or wrappers.** These are refused (`UNVERIFIABLE`), including honest ones (cases d2, h:
    57	  the two registered false rejects).
    58	- **One receipt per presentation.** A receipt id (SHA-256 of `A_sig`) names a receipt, not a presentation. Signing
    59	  is randomized, so the same honest frames can carry more than one valid signature set and therefore more than one
    60	  valid receipt, each with its own T. Each still meets G1–G10. (Owner, 2026-10-19, from finding E-02 of the first
    61	  real run.)
    62	- **Stability of T and U across different proofs.** A different proof or pin set can select a different block.
    63	- **Protection for a checker who skips authenticating the verifier release** or runs a substituted one.
    64	
    65	## 4. Failure model
    66	
    67	| Failure or attacker | What still holds |
    68	|---|---|
    69	| Tessera disappears | Everything in §2, if the holder kept the receipt and the verifier release. Reconstruction after the project dies is not demonstrated. |
    70	| Attacker alters a receipt or moves its signatures | Rejected (G1, G3, G10). |
    71	| Attacker uses its own keys, authority records not captured | Rejected: the required authority evidence does not name them (G4). |
    72	| Attacker captures both DNS and git authority records | Honest receipts protected (G10). New receipts under its own keys **pass** (the listing was cut). |
    73	| Attacker captures the issuing AWS account | **Not protected.** It can issue receipts that verify. |
    74	| Attacker steals the release key | Not protected: it can publish releases with a trust configuration of its choosing. This is distribution compromise, which A9 excludes (A9:47–52). A checker who installs no later release is unaffected. |
    75	| Bitcoin miners lie about time | G5 holds (chain order). G6 holds only under A-UTC. |
    76	| Bitcoin reorganizes fewer than 6 blocks | G5 holds. |
    77	
    78	## 5. Pairing: where each guarantee is checked
    79	
    80	The method needs every guarantee checked twice: once by a formal model or inspection, once by tests against the
    81	implementation. When the two disagree, we fix the contract, the model or the code. This is a first mapping, not
    82	yet checked case by case.
    83	
    84	**These are mappings, not pairs.** A pair, in the method's sense, runs the same scenario on both sides, so that a
    85	disagreement means something. Here the model side and the test side were written separately, for different
    86	scenarios, and none of the rows below runs one scenario through both. The tupay project's M1 found the same gap in
    87	its own workflow ("the pairs were not pairs", `../tupay/experiments/M1-result.md`). Making real pairs (shared
    88	scenarios, and a `derives: G…` tag on each case and model element) is post-freeze work. (Owner, 2026-10-21.)
    89	
    90	| # | Model side | Test side (`prototype/conformance/cases-index.json` groups) |
    91	|---|---|---|
    92	| G1 | P1, P8 (prose proof and vectors) | container, codec, field grammar, numbers, spec binding, receipt consistency, OTS |
    93	| G2 | P1 | A9.1 mapping |
    94	| G3 | P2, P3 | signature profile, possession, hybrid semantics, hybrid routing, hybrid positive, keys, receipt consistency |
    95	| G4 | P10, S-P3: abstract authority and key binding only | DNSSEC, DNS record, git, chain order, authority isolation, pinned D |
    96	| G5 | P5, P5c: temporal and confirmation rules. Existence before the block is an assumption, not modelled. | OTS, proofs, time |
    97	| G6 | none | UTC assessment (UTC-01..UTC-10: honest, nonmonotone ×2, 2106 boundary, lowest-height segment, reported with INVALID and UNVERIFIABLE, null with T null ×3); `prototype/indep-verifier/tests/test_utc_assessment.py` (11 tests, including the real-1 receipt's headers 972187..972192); `prototype/e2e/tests/test_driver_dry.py::test_dry_run_utc_assessment`. Built 2026-10-19 (profile §7.4). A-UTC itself is unchecked by design |
    98	| G7 | none (P5/P5c model related rules, not these endpoints) | time |
    99	| G8 | P9: contract and code inspection; no discharging model is required | offline reproducibility vectors not built (`release checker` tests configuration updates) |
   100	| G9 | P4 | dispatch, aggregation, A9.1 mapping, container (d2), trust config |
   101	| G10 | S-P3 (honest-material key binding) | none named; honest-material transplant tests to be identified or built |
   102	
   103	**Gaps this table shows.**
   104	- Model missing: G6 (tests added 2026-10-19; A-UTC is an assumption, not a modelled property), G7's endpoints, and
   105	  concrete authority (G4 beyond the abstract model).
   106	- Tests missing: G8's offline reproducibility vectors, G10's honest-material transplant cases.
   107	
   108	## 6. What the tester does (4 December)
   109	
   110	One independent tester, who meets the criteria in `docs/V1-TESTER-BRIEF.md`, takes a receipt bundle and the
   111	verifier release and runs the verifier. For each case in A9.1's scenario mapping, the tester decides from the
   112	documentation alone whether to rely on the receipt, with no help from the project. **Pass:** no unsafe accepts,
   113	and false rejects only on d2 and h. The result is that one tester's result.
   100	### keywords
   101	
   102	- Row 2, service-key listing, KEEP, CUT, attacker-selected keys, DNS, git authority, future issuance, pre-receipt commitment, listing-cut.md
   103	
   104	## User preferences
   105	
   106	- When beginning Tessera work, the user explicitly welcomes read-only exploration and questions before edits -> start with repository orientation and questions, not edits or commits. [Task 1]
   107	- The user wants mechanical review separated from author decisions -> label facts, model/test evidence, owner recommendations, pending rulings, adopted changes, and unverified claims separately. [Task 1]
   108	- The user dislikes context-assuming “cold reads” -> assemble the current promise, assumptions, open decisions, and consequences in plain language instead of making them reconstruct amendment tables. [Task 1]
   109	- The user wants anti-sycophantic review and precise claims: distinguish “bytes/metadata were preserved or changed” from claims about truth, authorship, or meaning. [Task 1]
   110	- For the E9 Row 2 choice, the user favored bounded staged scope: V1 complexity is already high, initial use is expected to be low, and the protection can be added for future issuance -> do not add complexity merely for a stronger theoretical guarantee; disclose the limitation precisely. [Task 2]
   111	
   112	## Reusable knowledge
   113	
   114	- Tessera’s central claim is binding exact presented bytes to signatures and a temporal anchor, not truth or submitter identity. Amend-don’t-rewrite applies: amendments outrank tracker summaries, and proposed/checked/discharged/owner-decided states must remain distinct. [Task 1]
   115	- Plain-language rendering and the prototype exposed gaps formal/document review missed: revocation lacked a source, relying parties could misread verdicts, byte layouts were underspecified, and Amendment 9 overclaimed Bitcoin header time. [Task 1]
   116	- Amendment 10’s service-key listing was cut in `docs/decisions/2026-10-19-listing-cut.md`; V1 therefore does not protect against new receipts under attacker-selected keys once both authority channels are captured. A receipt alone does not provide an authentic historical verifier/release package or trust provenance. [Task 1][Task 2]
   117	- KEEP would block fresh attacker-key receipts after both DNS and git authority capture only for a user with an authentic listed verifier; CUT knowingly permits them while honest receipts remain protected under registered assumptions. Future issuance can add the listing, but cannot retroactively establish its required pre-receipt commitment for already-issued receipts. The author CUT is an independent scope decision, not the registered schedule-slip cut order. [Task 2]
   118	- The retained HIGH R3-01 custody issue is that the reviewed build was not explicitly bound to the exact archive later signed/distributed. Green P8 vectors, end-to-end tests, independent-verifier tests, and a recorded 352/352 differential agreement are evidence of those checks, not a general service discharge. [Task 1]
   119	- The desired evidence model is preservation plus visible gaps: a chain cannot prove omitted history never existed, but expected anchors, links, or independently preserved records can make breaks visible. [Task 1]
